{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Vasicek1953-filter-15-cemented",
  "Source": "A. Vasicek, \"Multiple thin dielectric films as mirrors and interference filters,\" Czech. J. Phys. 3, 206-222 (1953). doi:10.1007/BF01687127 - Sec. III b-c, Fig. 7c (p. 218), Eqs. (49)-(55), Fig. 9 (p. 221), Table IV (p. 222)",
  "Notes": "All-dielectric 7+1+7 = 15 film Fabry-Perot interference filter (Fig. 7c): quarter-wave H/L mirrors around a half-wave low-index spacer, cemented between glass carrier and cover glass, transmission peak at 550 nm\nSpecification: Narrow transmission band at 550 nm, T = 100 % theoretically\nReported: Text pp. 220-221 and Fig. 9 (p. 221): theoretical T = 100 % at 550 nm (x' = 360 deg) when both sides are glass of the same index (rho15^2 = 0, Eq. 52 with m = r\"); side bands of high transmission remain (Fig. 9 shows T rising to ~0.83 at x' = 240 deg, 8250 A, and x' = 480 deg, 4125 A, read off the plot); Table IV text (p. 222) says the high mirror reflectance holds for x' about 300-420 deg (660-471 nm). Half-width: paper computes 83.1 A (cryolite spacer, r^2 = 0.95363 in Eq. 48) and 61.2 A for an air spacer; Polster measured 65 A and 80 % transmission for the 15-film filter. Caution: Eq. (48) treats each mirror as a fixed-phase reflector; an exact matrix calculation of this 15-layer stack gives a FWHM of about 3.67 nm (36.7 A) because of the mirror phase dispersion, so the 83.1 A value is not expected to be reproduced. Independent TMM check: T = 0.858 at 8250 A and 4125 A, T < 0.016 from 6600 to 4714 A outside the peak.\nNotes: Fig. 7c lists from the top D (cover glass) A B A B A B A, lambda/2 B, A B A B A B A, D (glass), i.e. H L H L H L H 2L H L H L H L H (15 layers); symmetric, so the order is the same either way. Cover glass and cement taken as the same index as the carrier glass (n = 1.518), as the paper assumes ('covered on both sides by glass (or by cement) of the same refractive index'). Spacer x' = 2x = 360 deg = half wave of index n2 (cryolite).\nLayers from Sec. III b-c, Fig. 7c (p. 218), Eqs. (49)-(55), Fig. 9 (p. 221), Table IV (p. 222); layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), agrees with the paper: ours T 100 % at 550 nm; paper T = 100 % at 550 nm.",
